ficates and publication of revocation status."],[372,"KMS deployments using the native PKCS#11 sidecar require Kubernetes 1.29 or newer. Prebuilt Lamassu container images are published for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64`, and the KMS PKCS#11 client module (`p11-kit-client.so`) is installed at the target architecture's Debian library path: `/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/pkcs11/p11-kit-client.so` on amd64 and `/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/pkcs11/p11-kit-client.so` on arm64. The x86\\_64 path is retained as a compatibility alias for existing configurations."],[1828,"KU, EKU and constraints permit the specific operation."],[366,"Kubernetes 1.24 or newer"],[2525,"Kubernetes derives a StatefulSet's PVC name from its pod name (`<volumeClaimTemplate>-<pod-name>`). Renaming the `kms` and `va` StatefulSets means `helm upgrade` binds brand-new, empty PVCs instead of the existing ones. The KMS key material and VA CRLs are not deleted — the old PVCs are orphaned, not removed — but the upgraded pods fail with `no such file or directory` (KMS) or `code=NotFound` (VA) until you copy the data across. This affects KMS when `services.kms.cryptoEngines` uses a `filesystem` engine and VA when `services.va.fileStore.type` is `local`. Every other renamed resource is stateless and is recreated without data-loss risk."],[32,"Kubernetes self-managed"],[316,"Kubernetes self-managed"],[893,"Label used in service logs."],[42,"Lamassu"],[1061,"Lamassu `JITP` creates inventory and identity in Lamassu. AWS JITP provisions a destination in AWS; these are separate decisions."],[2427,"Lamassu 4.0.0 is a major release. Storage consolidates into a single PostgreSQL database, a new `authz` service authorizes every request, the Helm chart is hardened, and several APIs and stored configurations change shape. Most data converts automatically during the upgrade, but some changes need operator action before, during or after it."],[475,"Lamassu access control turns an authenticated identity into concrete permission